#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# nanosoc_eth_chiplet — SpyGlass CDC Design Constraints (chiplet integration top)
#
# A joint work commissioned on behalf of SoC Labs, under Arm Academic Access
# license.
#
# Contributors
#
# David Mapstone (d.a.mapstone@soton.ac.uk)
#
# Copyright 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Nine blocks below this level each ship a `.sgdc` + `waiver.swl`. The INTEGRATED
# chiplet shipped none. The crossings this repository actually OWNS are the ones
# at the seams between those nine blocks — SoC fabric <-> D2D link, SoC fabric
# <-> RTC/PTP, SoC fabric <-> RMII/MII, SoC fabric <-> QSPI, SoC fabric <-> SWD —
# and until this file existed none of them had ever been looked at by a tool that
# understands asynchronous clock relationships. The block files cannot see them:
# every one of them stops at its own port list.
#
# This is a STARTING POINT, in the same sense as
# constraints/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_cdc.sdc. It is written to be reviewed before it
# is believed. Read cdc/README.md first — in particular the list of inconsistencies
# between the block-level files that this reconciliation had to resolve, and the
# list of what it still does not cover.
#
# TOP CHOICE: nanosoc_eth_chiplet, NOT nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip. Rationale in
# cdc/README.md §1. The short version: the generated chip wrapper ALIASES
# rtc_clk and user_ref_clk onto sys_fclk
# (build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:102-103), which collapses three of
# the domains this analysis exists to examine into one and would report a false
# clean. It also leaves role_locked_o open (:175), so the reset declaration in
# §3 below has nothing to bind to.
#
# EVERY port name in this file was checked against
# src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:48-213 (111 ports). Every hierarchical name was
# checked against the RTL it names, with file:line cited. Nothing here is
# inferred from a sibling file — that is exactly the mistake documented at
# tidelink/cdc/axi_chiplet_controller.sgdc:60-81, where three renamed ports were
# silently dropped, DEFAULTED to user_ref_clk, and manufactured two crossings
# that do not exist.
#
# THE RULE THIS FILE IS WRITTEN TO, and the reason several declarations below
# are absent rather than approximate:
#
#     AN ABSENT DECLARATION OVER-REPORTS. A WRONG DECLARATION MASKS.
#
# A port left unconstrained gets a virtual clock and shows up as a crossing you
# then have to explain — noisy, but every real crossing is still in the report.
# A port bound to the wrong clock is silently made same-domain, and a real
# crossing disappears from the report entirely. The two failure modes are not
# symmetric and must not be traded off as if they were. Where the launch clock
# of a port could not be established from the RTL, it is left in the explicit
# "not yet constrained" block (§6) or carries a TODO(cdc) — never a guess.
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

current_design nanosoc_eth_chiplet

# =============================================================================
# 1. CLOCK DEFINITIONS
# =============================================================================
# PERIOD SET: the ASIC chip SDC (ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/*.sdc with
# CLK_PERIOD=10.0 from ASIC/common.mk:183). The block-level .sgdc files disagree
# with each other AND with the chip — see cdc/README.md §3 for the full
# three-column reconciliation. Structural CDC (cdc_verify) does not use the
# period to decide whether a crossing exists, but a single self-consistent set
# is required so that (a) the domain names mean the same thing they mean in
# synthesis, and (b) the frequency-ratio-sensitive rules (Ac_conv, data-hold)
# are not fed contradictory numbers.
#
# The two numbers below that are NOT from the chip SDC are marked [OWNER],
# following the convention in constraints/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_cdc.sdc: the chip
# SDC does not create them at all, because the pad-level wrapper aliases both
# onto the system clock.

# --- Fabric ------------------------------------------------------------------
# sys_fclk: free-running SoC clock, chiplet input port.
#   ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/constraints.sdc:61 (create_clock "clk" on pad CLK)
#   ASIC/common.mk:183                          (CLK_PERIOD ?= 10.0)
clock -name sys_fclk  -domain sys_fclk_domain -period 10.0

# sys_hclk: the AHB fabric clock. It is an OUTPUT PORT of this top
# (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:59) generated inside the SoC's PRMU and routed
# back out (nanosoc_multicore_soc.sv:619-622). It clocks essentially the whole
# integration wrapper: u_d2d_decode (:653), u_tlapb_bridge, u_tcapb_bridge,
# u_tidelink.hclk (:762) and u_tidechart.clk (:971). Declaring it is not
# optional — leave it out and every flop in this file's own logic lands in a
# virtual domain.
#
# SAME DOMAIN as sys_fclk deliberately. sys_hclk is synchronous to sys_fclk (it
# is derived from it inside the PRMU); the exact divide ratio is the same
# [OWNER] item the integration SDC records at
# constraints/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_cdc.sdc:65-71. For CDC the DOMAIN is what
# matters and the domain is not in doubt. If the ratio ever turns out to be
# something other than an integer divide with a deterministic phase, this line
# is the one to revisit.
clock -name sys_hclk  -domain sys_fclk_domain -period 10.0

# --- Ethernet ----------------------------------------------------------------
# rmii_ref_clk: 50 MHz RMII reference from the PHY. Chiplet input port.
#   ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/ethernet_constraints.sdc:19,23
clock -name rmii_ref_clk -domain rmii_domain -period 20.0

# THE TWO MII CLOCKS. These are the domains the existing integration SDC misses
# entirely (constraints/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_cdc.sdc declares rmii_ref_clk and
# stops). They are NOT ports at any level — u_rmii_to_mii generates them by
# toggling two output registers, so they are rmii_ref_clk / 2 = 25 MHz.
#
# Everything downstream of them is a genuine asynchronous domain against the
# FABRIC: the OpenCores MAC core, the RX checksum snoop (eth_rx_cksum) and both
# PTP event detectors run here while their register sides run on sys_hclk. That
# fabric<->MII boundary is the crossing worth finding.
#
# BUT THEY ARE ONE DOMAIN, NOT TWO — AND THAT DOMAIN IS rmii_domain.
# Every block-level file in the tree declares a separate mtx_domain and
# mrx_domain (ethmac_ahb.sgdc:27-30, ethmac_subsystem_apb.sgdc:28-31,
# ethernet_ss_ahb.sgdc:32-35, nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:44-45). That is CORRECT
# at their scope — ethmac_ahb takes mtx_clk_pad_i / mrx_clk_pad_i as ports from
# a real MII PHY, which sources two genuinely independent clocks. It is WRONG
# here. This chiplet is RMII: one 50 MHz reference generates both halves.
#
# Verified directly in the source this build compiles
# (nanosoc-multicore-system/ethernet-subsystem-ahb/ethernet-mac-ahb/
#  amba_wb_bridges/src/rtl/rmii_to_mii.v, per build/chip/flist/soc.flist:163):
#     :277-283  always @(posedge rmii_ref_clk or negedge rstn_sync)
#                   mrx_clk <= 1'b0 / ~mrx_clk   under `if (tick)`
#     :320-326  always @(posedge rmii_ref_clk or negedge rstn_sync)
#                   mtx_clk <= 1'b0 / ~mtx_clk   under `if (tick)`
# Same launch edge, same async reset (rstn_sync), same enable (`tick`, :152),
# same reset value. The two waveforms are bit-identical by construction — they
# cannot drift, cannot skew relative to each other, and cannot present a
# metastability window to one another. Declaring them as separate asynchronous
# domains would MANUFACTURE mtx<->mrx crossings that are physically impossible,
# and would bury the real fabric<->MII findings under them.
#
# They are likewise synchronous to rmii_ref_clk itself (deterministic /2 off the
# same edge), so all three sit in rmii_domain. This matches how the chip SDC
# groups them: `-group [get_clocks {rmii_ref_clk mii_rx_clk mii_tx_clk}]`
# (ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/constraints.sdc:192) — one async group, i.e. one
# CDC domain.
#
# They are still declared as CLOCK OBJECTS (not folded away) because the flops
# they drive genuinely toggle at 25 MHz, and the fabric<->MII crossings must be
# attributed to the right launch clock.
#
# Hierarchical anchor verified:
#   nanosoc_eth_chiplet.u_soc                (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:515)
#     -> nanosoc_multicore_soc.u_network_core (nanosoc_multicore_soc.sv:859)
#       -> ethernet_ss_ahb_rmii.u_rmii_to_mii (ethernet_ss_ahb_rmii.sv:555)
#          output pins .mtx_clk / .mrx_clk    (ethernet_ss_ahb_rmii.sv:563,567)
# Same anchor the chip SDC uses, with the pad-ring prefix stripped:
#   ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/ethernet_constraints.sdc:20-21,41-42
# If SpyGlass cannot bind a pin name here, the driving NETS one level up are
# u_soc/u_network_core/mtx_clk_w and .../mrx_clk_w (ethernet_ss_ahb_rmii.sv:563,567).
clock -name "u_soc/u_network_core/u_rmii_to_mii/mtx_clk" -domain rmii_domain -period 40.0
clock -name "u_soc/u_network_core/u_rmii_to_mii/mrx_clk" -domain rmii_domain -period 40.0

# --- RTC / PTP ---------------------------------------------------------------
# rtc_clk: HA1588 PTP time-base reference. Chiplet input port, asynchronous to
# the fabric. [OWNER] period — the chip SDC creates NO clock for this because
# the pad wrapper aliases rtc_clk onto sys_fclk
# (build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:102). 30.518 ns is the value the
# integration SDC carries (constraints/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_cdc.sdc:42).
clock -name rtc_clk -domain rtc_domain -period 30.518

# --- QSPI --------------------------------------------------------------------
# QSPI serial clock. Generated INSIDE the SoC by ahb_qspi's divider and brought
# out on the qspi_sclk chiplet output port (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:133).
#
# Declared at the top-level OUTPUT PORT, following the house precedent at
# nanosoc-multicore-system/cdc/nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:55-60. If domain
# propagation back to the divider fails, swap this line for the verified
# hierarchical anchor (the chip SDC's, with the pad-ring prefix stripped):
#   u_soc/u_qspi_flash_0/u_top_ahb_qspi/u_qspi_clock_div/QSPI_SCLK_i
#   (nanosoc_multicore_soc.sv:1092; src/rtl/wrappers/qspi_flash_ahb.v:105;
#    ahb_qspi/logical/top_ahb_qspi/logical/top_ahb_qspi.v:443,447;
#    ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/qspi_constraints.sdc:2)
# Period 20.0 = clk / 2, the divide the chip SDC declares.
#
# ---- B7-ROUND2 CORRECTION: sys_fclk_domain, NOT a separate qspi_domain ------
# The first run declared this `-domain qspi_domain`, on the reasoning that "the
# divider is programmable, so the phase relationship to the fabric is not
# guaranteed". That reasoning is wrong, and it MANUFACTURED four crossings
# (B7 baseline §6 group B, findings 31/37/47/48). The divider RTL is
# nanosoc-multicore-system/ahb_qspi/logical/qspi_controller/logical/qspi_clock_div.v:
#
#   :10  assign QSPI_SCLK_i = (QSPI_CLK_DIV==5'h00) ? HCLK : QSPI_SCLK_reg;
#   :13  always @(posedge HCLK or negedge HRESETn) ...
#   :19,23   QSPI_SCLK_reg <= ~QSPI_SCLK_reg;
#
# Both arms of that mux are HCLK: either HCLK itself, or a register that only
# ever toggles on a posedge of HCLK. Every QSPI_SCLK edge is therefore an HCLK
# edge. A programmable INTEGER divide off a single launch edge changes the
# frequency; it does not create an independent oscillator and cannot present a
# metastability window to the fabric. This is a synchronously-derived clock and
# it belongs in sys_fclk_domain — which is exactly what the chip SDC already
# says (ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/qspi_constraints.sdc:2, QSPI_SCLK = clk / 2,
# a create_generated_clock, not a create_clock).
#
# It stays a declared CLOCK OBJECT rather than being folded away, so that the
# flops it launches are still attributed to it and STA still owns the divided
# path. Only the DOMAIN changes.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM: it says nothing about setup/hold on the divided
# path, and nothing about the qspi_io_i pad RETURN path being fast enough. Both
# are STA questions and the chip SDC owns them. See also §5, where the four
# qspi_* pads are declared against this clock.
clock -name qspi_sclk -domain sys_fclk_domain -period 20.0

# --- Debug -------------------------------------------------------------------
# dap_swclktck: SWJ-DP SWCLK/TCK, driven by an external probe. Asynchronous by
# specification. 40.0 = 4 x CLK_PERIOD
# (ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/constraints.sdc:20,62).
clock -name dap_swclktck -domain swclktck_domain -period 40.0

# --- Die-to-die link ---------------------------------------------------------
# user_ref_clk: the Wlink PLL reference. Chiplet input port, asynchronous to the
# fabric. [OWNER] period — as with rtc_clk the chip SDC creates no clock here
# (the pad wrapper aliases it onto sys_fclk,
# build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:103, which is exactly the aliasing
# that makes `_chip` the wrong top for this analysis). 8.0 ns is the value the
# integration SDC carries (constraints/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_cdc.sdc:32).
clock -name user_ref_clk -domain wlink_ref_domain -period 8.0

# pad_clk_rx: the FAR die's forwarded transmit clock, recovered at our RX pads.
# A real primary clock and asynchronous to EVERYTHING on this die — it only
# toggles when the other die is powered and transmitting (docs/RESET_ORDERING.md
# §2; tidelink_top.sv:235). Period from the chip SDC's D2D_RX_CLK_0, which is
# $EXTCLK_PERIOD by the mesochronous-shared-source argument at
# ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc:1-38.
clock -name pad_clk_rx -domain phy_rx_domain -period 10.0

# pad_clk_tx: our own forwarded TX clock, divide-by-1 off user_ref_clk
# (ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc:43). Same domain as its
# source — this is a source-synchronous forward, not an independent clock.
clock -name pad_clk_tx -domain wlink_ref_domain -period 8.0

# idelay_ref_clk: 200 MHz IDELAYCTRL reference. FPGA target only — the ASIC
# build ties this to 0 (build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:101,
# USE_IDELAY=0). Declared as a clock in its own domain anyway, matching
# tidelink/cdc/tidelink_top.sgdc:101-103, because a declared clock produces
# crossings to examine while a tied constant produces silence. On an ASIC-only
# run this domain will simply be empty.
clock -name idelay_ref_clk -domain idelay_domain -period 5.0

# =============================================================================
# 2. RESET DEFINITIONS
# =============================================================================
# NAMING NOTE. The three reset regimes TideLink distinguishes (poresetn,
# hresetn, and the role_locked bring-up gate — docs/RESET_ORDERING.md §1) do NOT
# appear at this boundary under those names. At this level:
#   tidelink_top.hresetn    <- sys_hresetn   (nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:763)
#   tidelink_top.poresetn   <- sys_poresetn  (nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:764)
#   tidelink_top.phc_resetn <- sys_hresetn   (nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:766)
# so there is no distinct `phc_resetn` object to declare here at all: the PHC
# shares the AHB reset (and the AHB clock, :765) in this build. That is a real
# difference from tidelink/cdc/tidelink_top.sgdc, which declares phc_resetn and
# a separate phc_clk domain because standalone TideLink has them as ports.

# Chiplet reset input.
reset -name sys_sysresetn -value 0

# PRMU-derived resets. Both are OUTPUT PORTS of this top
# (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:58,60) driven from inside u_soc, and both are
# consumed by this file's own integration logic — so they must be declared here,
# not left to propagate from wherever SpyGlass finds them.
reset -name sys_poresetn  -value 0
reset -name sys_hresetn   -value 0

# Debug reset inputs.
reset -name dap_ntrst   -value 0
reset -name dap_npotrst -value 0

# --- role_locked_o AS A RESET ------------------------------------------------
# This is the non-obvious one, and it is load-bearing. role_locked_o is an
# OUTPUT of this top (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:209) — a status bit by its
# name — but functionally it is the third reset regime of the D2D link, and
# declaring it as a reset is what tidelink/cdc/tidelink_top.sgdc:47-50 does.
#
# It gates BOTH reset domains of the a2l (app-to-link) replay ACK-pointer CDC,
# so that the app side and the link side release on the SAME bring-up event
# instead of on their own resets (docs/RESET_ORDERING.md §1):
#     axi_chiplet_controller.sv:2681  wire wlink_por_reset = ~poresetn | ~role_locked;
#     axi_chiplet_controller.sv:2686  assign app_clk_reset = ~hresetn  | ~role_locked;
# Without the ~role_locked term the two sides leave reset at different times and
# the gray ACK-pointer synchroniser desyncs — the asymmetric reset-RELEASE skew
# the fix at :443-449 exists to close.
#
# It is ALSO the only release path for the entire recovered-RX-clock domain:
# async-asserted and sync-deasserted into the recovered clock at
# axi_chiplet_controller.sv:3542, and wired straight (no inverter) into the
# gpio-phy register slave at tidelink_top.sv:981,919-921.
#
# If SpyGlass does not know this is a reset, every flop in the recovered-RX
# domain and both halves of the a2l CDC are analysed as if they were released by
# their local resets, which is precisely the wrong model.
reset -name role_locked_o -value 0

# =============================================================================
# 3. DFT CONSTANTS
# =============================================================================
# All seven are TIED in this build — they are constants, not slow-moving
# signals. build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:119-123 ties scan_mode,
# scan_asyncrst_ctrl, scan_clk, scan_shift and scan_in to 0; :40-41,83-84 bond
# sys_scanenable and sys_testmode to pads that are strapped inactive in mission
# mode.
#
# set_case_analysis, NOT quasi_static. Case analysis propagates the constant and
# DELETES the scan-only logic from the analysis; quasi_static merely says "this
# does not change often", which leaves the scan muxes in the netlist and lets
# them carry paths between domains that mission mode can never take.
#
# RESOLVED INCONSISTENCY: the ethernet subsystem declares two of these
# quasi_static instead —
#   nanosoc-multicore-system/ethernet-subsystem-ahb/cdc/ethernet_ss_ahb.sgdc:54-55
#     quasi_static -name sys_testmode
#     quasi_static -name sys_scanenable
# with the comment that without them SpyGlass heuristically assigns the ports to
# mrx_clk_i and reports three false unsync crossings into the M0+ PRMU clock-
# enable logic. That symptom is real; the cure is under-strength. Case analysis
# fixes the same three and additionally removes the scan-mux paths. The SoC-level
# file already reached the same conclusion for these two ports
# (nanosoc-multicore-system/cdc/nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:80-81). See
# cdc/README.md §4.
set_case_analysis -name scan_mode          -value 0
set_case_analysis -name scan_asyncrst_ctrl -value 0
set_case_analysis -name scan_clk           -value 0
set_case_analysis -name scan_shift         -value 0
set_case_analysis -name scan_in            -value 0
set_case_analysis -name sys_scanenable     -value 0
set_case_analysis -name sys_testmode       -value 0

# =============================================================================
# 4. QUASI-STATIC STRAPS
# =============================================================================
# Genuine straps: pad-ring or OTP values, stable before reset deassertion and
# never re-driven during functional operation. Unlike the DFT pins above these
# are NOT tied in RTL — a real pad ring chooses them per die — so a constant is
# the wrong model and quasi_static is the right one.

# Link role strap. Also overloaded as TideChart's root-election tie-break
# (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:994).
quasi_static -name "role_strap_i"

# PUF seed + auto-negotiation priority. Sourced from OTP or a die UID; the
# per-die tiebreak the auto-neg root election needs (docs/RESET_ORDERING.md §3.4).
quasi_static -name "puf_seed*"
quasi_static -name "nego_priority_i*"

# Bring-up straps that open the software-driven role-lock path
# (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:176-180).
quasi_static -name "mask_hs_bypass_i"

# I2C sideband pads. Truly asynchronous external signals; synchronised inside
# the chiplet-controller black box by dedicated WavDemetReset cells (the
# i2c_*_sync blocks at the top of axi_chiplet_controller.sv). quasi_static here
# says "do not invent a launch domain for these", not "do not check them".
quasi_static -name "i2c_scl_i"
quasi_static -name "i2c_sda_i"

# SWJ-DP enable. Tied 1 in this single-chiplet build
# (build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:106); a multi-chiplet package would
# pin it 0 on inactive dies to quiesce the SWD line.
#
# DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from nanosoc-multicore-system/cdc/nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:86,
# which uses `set_case_analysis -name dap_swj_enable -value 1`. Case analysis
# would constant-propagate the SWD path permanently ON and remove the muxing
# that a multi-chiplet build relies on. It is a configuration strap, not a DFT
# tie, so it belongs here. Noted in cdc/README.md §4.
quasi_static -name "dap_swj_enable"

# --- Additions beyond the required set, argued individually ------------------
# sys_sysresetreq: a pulse from an external manager, held 0 and pulsed to
# request a reset; tied 0 in this build
# (build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:104). Same treatment and same
# reasoning as nanosoc-multicore-system/cdc/nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:155.
quasi_static -name "sys_sysresetreq"

# apb_debug_unlock_i / puf_ready: pad straps at THIS boundary
# (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:184,186), tied 0 in the chip wrapper (:115,128).
#
# DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from tidelink/cdc/tidelink_top.sgdc:98-99 and
# tidelink/cdc/axi_chiplet_controller.sgdc:83-84, both of which declare these
# hclk-launched. That is correct in standalone TideLink, where the bench drives
# them from SoC-clocked logic. It is NOT correct here: at the chiplet boundary
# they arrive from the pad ring with no launch clock at all, and claiming an
# hclk launch would assert a synchronous relationship that the package does not
# provide. Noted in cdc/README.md §4.
quasi_static -name "apb_debug_unlock_i"
quasi_static -name "puf_ready"

# =============================================================================
# 5. BOUNDARY PORT CENSUS  (abstract_port)
# =============================================================================
# THE HIGHEST-RISK SECTION IN THIS FILE, and the reason it is a census rather
# than a list. tidelink/cdc/axi_chiplet_controller.sgdc:60-81 records what
# happens when a boundary port list drifts from the RTL: names that no longer
# exist are FATAL (SGDC_abstract_port02 -> exit 7 -> the "0 clocks / 0 crossings"
# early termination), and names that were never added are silently DEFAULTED to
# whichever clock SpyGlass picks, manufacturing crossings that do not exist. Two
# spurious Ac_unsync01 violations came from exactly that omission.
#
# So: all 111 ports of src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:48-213 are accounted for
# below. Ports handled by §1-§4 (clocks, resets, DFT constants, straps) are
# named in comments where they fall, so a reviewer can tick off the whole port
# list without cross-referencing. Nothing is silently absent.

# --- System / fabric ---------------------------------------------------------
#   sys_fclk        -> §1 clock         sys_sysresetn -> §2 reset
#   sys_hclk        -> §1 clock         sys_poresetn  -> §2 reset
#   sys_hresetn     -> §2 reset         sys_scanenable/sys_testmode -> §3
#   sys_sysresetreq -> §4 quasi_static

# --- External ethernet-subsystem AHB slave (11 ports) ------------------------
# Testbench / external-manager access into the eth-ss. Driven externally in the
# fabric domain; tied inactive in the chip wrapper
# (build/chip/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:129-136) but a real port here.
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_haddr[*]"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_htrans[*]"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hwrite"      -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hsize[*]"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hburst[*]"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hprot[*]"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hwdata[*]"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hmastlock"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hrdata[*]"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hready"      -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "eth_ss_0_hresp"       -clock sys_hclk

# --- CPU sideband (16 ports) -------------------------------------------------
# Both cores run on sys_hclk (the PRMU-divided fabric clock), so these are
# fabric-domain in and out. NOTE the port names here are network_core_* /
# chip_core_*, NOT the cpu_0_* / cpu_1_* that
# nanosoc-multicore-system/cdc/nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:98-103 uses — see
# cdc/README.md §4, those SoC-level names do not exist on the SoC top either.
abstract_port -ports "network_core_pmuenable"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_pmuenable"       -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "network_core_nmi"          -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "network_core_txev"         -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "network_core_rxev"         -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "network_core_lockup"       -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "network_core_sysresetreq"  -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "network_core_sleeping"     -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "network_core_sleepdeep"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_nmi"             -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_txev"            -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_rxev"            -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_lockup"          -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_sysresetreq"     -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_sleeping"        -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_sleepdeep"       -clock sys_hclk

# --- Debug access port (SWJ-DP / JTAG) ---------------------------------------
#   dap_swclktck -> §1 clock   dap_ntrst / dap_npotrst -> §2 resets
#   dap_swj_enable -> §4 quasi_static
# The remaining six are the serial-wire / JTAG pins, launched and captured by
# the probe clock. This is the domain boundary the DAP's own CDC cells
# (cm0p_dap_dp_cdc.v, cm0p_dap_ap_cdc.v, cm0p_dap_cdc_capt_sync.v) exist to
# cross; they are NOT black-boxed here, so those should return as recognised
# synchronisers rather than as violations.
abstract_port -ports "dap_swditms"  -clock dap_swclktck
abstract_port -ports "dap_swdo"     -clock dap_swclktck
abstract_port -ports "dap_swdoen"   -clock dap_swclktck
abstract_port -ports "dap_tdi"      -clock dap_swclktck
abstract_port -ports "dap_tdo"      -clock dap_swclktck
abstract_port -ports "dap_ntdoen"   -clock dap_swclktck

# --- RMII PHY ----------------------------------------------------------------
#   rmii_ref_clk -> §1 clock
# RMII is source-synchronous to the reference clock in both directions, so all
# four data/control pins belong to it. They are NOT in the MII domains: the
# rmii_to_mii bridge is the boundary, and it sits INSIDE u_network_core.
abstract_port -ports "rmii_txd[*]"   -clock rmii_ref_clk
abstract_port -ports "rmii_tx_en"    -clock rmii_ref_clk
abstract_port -ports "rmii_rxd[*]"   -clock rmii_ref_clk
abstract_port -ports "rmii_crs_dv"   -clock rmii_ref_clk

# --- MDIO --------------------------------------------------------------------
# MDC is generated by the MAC from the fabric clock and the PHY returns MDIO
# synchronous to the MDC we ourselves drive, so the whole group is fabric-domain.
# (nanosoc-multicore-system/cdc/nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:141-147 reaches the
# same conclusion.)
abstract_port -ports "md_pad_i"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "mdc_pad_o"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "md_pad_o"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "md_padoe_o"  -clock sys_hclk

# --- UART / watchdog ---------------------------------------------------------
# Transmit sides and the watchdog reset are fabric-launched.
abstract_port -ports "uart_txd"             -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_uart_txd"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "chip_core_wdog_reset" -clock sys_hclk

# --- RTC / PTP outputs -------------------------------------------------------
#   rtc_clk -> §1 clock
# The three rtc_time_* outputs come from the HA1588 RTC, which is clocked by
# rtc_clk (ethernet_ss_ahb_rmii.sv:515-518). This IS the rtc_domain -> fabric
# crossing the integration owns.
abstract_port -ports "rtc_time_ptp_ns[*]"   -clock rtc_clk
abstract_port -ports "rtc_time_ptp_sec[*]"  -clock rtc_clk
abstract_port -ports "rtc_time_one_pps"     -clock rtc_clk

# --- Ethernet / PHC interrupts + status --------------------------------------
# CORRECTION TO THE SoC-LEVEL FILE. nanosoc_multicore_soc.sgdc:125-127 puts
# phc_pps_out, phc_pps_irq and phc_alarm_irq in rtc_clk. They are not: the PHC
# is instantiated as phc_ahb with .HCLK(u_network_core_sys_hclk)
# (nanosoc_multicore_soc.sv:1036-1038) and pps_out is its output (:1074). The
# PHC is a single-clock design on the fabric clock — that is what its own block
# file says too (ptp-hardware-clock-ahb/cdc/phc_ahb.sgdc:24, "all logic runs on
# hclk"). Declaring them rtc_clk here would invent a crossing into
# u_tidelink.phc_pps (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:855) that does not exist,
# and would hide the fact that the real rtc->fabric crossing is upstream, inside
# the HA1588 servo. See cdc/README.md §4.
abstract_port -ports "eth_irq"        -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "phc_pps_out"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "phc_pps_irq"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "phc_alarm_irq"  -clock sys_hclk
# ha1588_servo_locked: driven by u_ha1588_servo, which is clocked by pclk
# (ethmac_subsystem_apb.v:620-623,653) and pclk is the fabric clock in this
# build. Fabric-domain, despite the HA1588 name.
abstract_port -ports "ha1588_servo_locked" -clock sys_hclk

# --- QSPI flash --------------------------------------------------------------
#   qspi_sclk -> §1 clock
# The pads are launched/captured by the generated serial clock.
abstract_port -ports "qspi_csn"     -clock qspi_sclk
abstract_port -ports "qspi_io_o[*]" -clock qspi_sclk
abstract_port -ports "qspi_io_i[*]" -clock qspi_sclk
abstract_port -ports "qspi_io_e[*]" -clock qspi_sclk

# --- PL022 SPI ---------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN LIMITATION, stated rather than hidden. The PL022 divides the fabric
# clock down to SSPCLKOUT internally; the correct model is a generated clock on
# that divider output, exactly as QSPI gets above. That anchor has NOT been
# verified in the RTL, so it is not declared — guessing a hierarchical pin is
# how the trap at the top of this section starts. Declaring the group
# fabric-domain is the conservative interim: it will NOT report the
# fabric<->SSPCLKOUT crossing, so do not read a clean SPI result as coverage.
# None of these four is bonded on the current chip wrapper
# (nanosoc_eth_chiplet_chip.v:145-147 leave three open, spi_miso tied at :126).
# TODO(cdc): declare the PL022 SSPCLKOUT divider as a generated clock and move
#            these four into it. Tracked in cdc/README.md §7.
abstract_port -ports "spi_sclk"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "spi_mosi"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "spi_ss[*]"  -clock sys_hclk

# --- HOSTIO4 P1 --------------------------------------------------------------
# GPIO. Outputs are fabric-launched.
abstract_port -ports "hostio4_p1_out[*]"    -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "hostio4_p1_outen[*]"  -clock sys_hclk

# --- TideLink GPIO-PHY pads --------------------------------------------------
#   pad_clk_tx / pad_clk_rx / user_ref_clk / idelay_ref_clk -> §1 clocks
# TX data is launched by our forwarded clock; RX data is recovered against the
# far die's forwarded clock. These two lines are the D2D data plane.
abstract_port -ports "pad_tx[*]"  -clock pad_clk_tx
abstract_port -ports "pad_rx[*]"  -clock pad_clk_rx

# --- TideLink I2C sideband ---------------------------------------------------
#   i2c_scl_i / i2c_sda_i -> §4 quasi_static (truly asynchronous inputs)
# The drive and output-enable sides are launched by the I2C controller inside
# the chiplet-controller black box, which runs on app_clk = sys_hclk.
abstract_port -ports "i2c_scl_o"  -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "i2c_scl_t"  -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "i2c_sda_o"  -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "i2c_sda_t"  -clock sys_hclk

# --- Link bring-up straps ----------------------------------------------------
#   role_strap_i, nego_priority_i, mask_hs_bypass_i, apb_debug_unlock_i,
#   puf_seed, puf_ready -> §4 quasi_static

# --- DFT ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#   scan_mode, scan_asyncrst_ctrl, scan_clk, scan_shift, scan_in -> §3 constants
# scan_out is the capture end of the chain. With the scan inputs case-analysed
# to 0 it carries no functional path; declared fabric-domain so it is not left
# to a default.
abstract_port -ports "scan_out" -clock sys_hclk

# --- Link status / observability ---------------------------------------------
#   role_locked_o -> §2 reset (see the argument there)
# link_active_o is tc_link_active (nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:496), which is
# tidelink_top.link_active — app-clk domain. role_is_master_o, d2d_reset_o,
# servo_locked_o and tl_ewma_credit_o likewise leave TideLink on hclk; the
# chiplet-controller boundary constraints in §7 pin the ones that come through
# the black box. tidechart_irq_o is tc_tidechart_irq from u_tidechart, clocked
# .clk(sys_hclk) (nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:971).
abstract_port -ports "link_active_o"      -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "d2d_reset_o"        -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "role_is_master_o"   -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "servo_locked_o"     -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "tl_ewma_credit_o[*]" -clock sys_hclk
abstract_port -ports "tidechart_irq_o"    -clock sys_hclk

# =============================================================================
# 6. DELIBERATELY NOT abstract_port'd — genuinely asynchronous pads
# =============================================================================
# These four are the only boundary ports with NO launch clock at all. Binding
# them to sys_hclk would make them same-domain, SpyGlass would report nothing,
# and a real crossing would be hidden behind a constraint rather than closed by
# a synchroniser. Left undeclared on purpose: SpyGlass assigns each a virtual
# clock (SG_VCLK_*) and reports the crossing, which the receiving IP's existing
# 2-flop synchroniser should then answer as RECOGNISED.
#
#   uart_rxd            serial RX into cmsdk_apb_uart; 2-FF sync at
#                       BP210 .../cmsdk_apb_uart/verilog/cmsdk_apb_uart.v:499
#                       (rxd_sync_1 <= RXD). Tied 1 in the chip wrapper (:124).
#   chip_core_uart_rxd  same cell, CPU1's UART. Tied 1 (:125).
#   spi_miso            PL022 receive pin. Sampled against the PL022's own
#                       SSPCLKOUT, which is not declared (see the SPI note in
#                       §5); until it is, this is honestly asynchronous.
#                       Tied 0 (:126).
#   hostio4_p1_in[*]    GPIO inputs, driven by whatever is on the board.
#
# If the first run buries the real findings under noise from these four, the fix
# is to declare the PL022 clock and confirm the UART synchronisers are
# recognised — NOT to bind them to the fabric clock.

# =============================================================================
# 7. BLACK-BOXED IP — PORT-LEVEL CLOCK DOMAINS
# =============================================================================
# Modules listed in `set_option stop_module` in cdc/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.prj.
# Every port of a stopped module needs a domain or SpyGlass defaults it. These
# sections are kept in THIS file rather than read from tidelink/cdc/*.sgdc so
# that (a) there is one period set, not two, and (b) the port lists could be
# re-verified against the RTL this build actually compiles — which turned up
# eleven ports the TideLink block file has since fallen behind on. See
# cdc/README.md §4.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7a. axi_chiplet_controller  (TideLink's Wlink-based D2D controller)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source compiled by this build:
#   tidelink/src/rtl/local_overrides/axi_chiplet_controller.sv
#   (build/chip/flist/tidelink_asic.flist:187 — the LOCAL OVERRIDE, not the deps
#    copy; see flist/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_asic.flist for why that distinction is
#    load-bearing.)
#
# Port list re-derived from that file's module header, not copied from
# tidelink/cdc/axi_chiplet_controller.sgdc. Ports marked [+] are ones the
# TideLink block file does not enumerate; each would otherwise be DEFAULTED and
# could manufacture a crossing, which is the exact defect that file's own
# comment at :60-81 describes.
current_design axi_chiplet_controller

# Clocks at the black-box boundary. Both apb_clk and app_clk are tied to hclk at
# the instantiation (tidelink_top.sv:2815-2816) and user_hsclk to user_ref_clk
# (:2817), so one app_clk object in the fabric domain models both.
clock -name app_clk -domain sys_fclk_domain -period 10.0

# The recovered RX WORD clock. Generated inside the controller as pad_clk_rx
# divided by 16 and exposed on link_rx_clk_o (axi_chiplet_controller.sv:3530;
# consumed at tidelink_top.sv:3091). Named after the output port so the alias
# resolves inside the black box, exactly as
# tidelink/cdc/axi_chiplet_controller.sgdc:24-30 does it.
#
# Period 160.0 = 16 x 10.0, matching the chip SDC's D2D_RX_WORD_CLK_n
# (ASIC/genus-innovus/inputs/tidelink_constraints.sdc:124-125, -divide_by 16 off
# D2D_RX_CLK_0). The TideLink block file says 64.0 because it is working from a
# 4.0 ns hclk; same divide, different base. This is one of the period
# disagreements cdc/README.md §3 reconciles.
clock -name link_rx_clk_o -domain link_rx_domain -period 160.0

# --- app_clk (= sys_hclk) domain ---------------------------------------------
# TideLink datapath and PTP words.
abstract_port -ports "tidelink_out[*]"      -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "tidelink_in[*]"       -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "ptp_out[*]"           -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "ptp_in[*]"            -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "tx_link_idle"         -clock app_clk

# AXI initiator / target.
abstract_port -ports "axi_ini_0_*"          -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "axi_tgt_0_*"          -clock app_clk

# APB register port.
abstract_port -ports "apb_psel"             -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_paddr[*]"         -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_penable"          -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_pprot[*]"         -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_pstrb[*]"         -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_pwrite"           -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_pwdata[*]"        -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_prdata[*]"        -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_pready"           -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_pslverr"          -clock app_clk

# Control-register side-channel. The apb_ prefix on write/addr/wdata is the
# integ override's rename that the TideLink file documents at :60-65.
abstract_port -ports "apb_ctrl_reg_write"    -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_ctrl_reg_addr[*]"  -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_ctrl_reg_wdata[*]" -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_ctrl_reg_r10"      -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "apb_ctrl_reg_rd"       -clock app_clk
# [+] apb_ctrl_reg_rf — AXI data-node OBS Region-F select. Port declared at
#     axi_chiplet_controller.sv:254, immediately after its r10 (:242) and rd
#     (:247) siblings and "exactly mirroring" them per the RTL comment at :251.
#     Driven from u_tidelink_fifo's ctrl_reg_rf output (tidelink_top.sv:2017),
#     that instance is clocked .hclk(hclk), so it is an hclk-domain sibling of
#     the write/addr/wdata strobes. NOT enumerated in the TideLink block file:
#     the identical omission that produced the two spurious Ac_unsync01
#     crossings recorded there at :69-79, recurring on a third port.
abstract_port -ports "apb_ctrl_reg_rf"       -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "ctrl_reg_rdata[*]"     -clock app_clk

# General bus.
abstract_port -ports "generalbus_out[*]"     -clock app_clk
# [+] generalbus_in — tied 32'h0 at the instantiation (tidelink_top.sv:2971),
#     so harmless today, but an unbound input on a stopped module is still a
#     default waiting to happen.
abstract_port -ports "generalbus_in[*]"      -clock app_clk

# Sideband reset / wake.
abstract_port -ports "sb_reset_out"          -clock app_clk
# [+] sb_reset_in  — tied 1'b0 (tidelink_top.sv:2822).
# [+] sb_wake      — left open (tidelink_top.sv:2824).
abstract_port -ports "sb_reset_in"           -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "sb_wake"               -clock app_clk

# Role resolution.
abstract_port -ports "role_is_master_o"      -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "role_locked_o"         -clock app_clk

# Interrupts.
abstract_port -ports "i2c_nbsy_irq"          -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "i2c_nrd_empty_irq"     -clock app_clk

# --- B7-ROUND2 ADDITION: the four I2C drive/enable pins ----------------------
# [+] The first run declared i2c_nbsy_irq above but NOT these four. With no
#     abstract_port SpyGlass DEFAULTED them to user_ref_clk, while §5 declares
#     the matching chiplet ports sys_hclk — so the tool reported a
#     user_ref_clk -> sys_hclk crossing on four wires that carry no logic at all
#     between the two ends. B7 baseline §6 group A, findings 2-5. This is the
#     THIRD recurrence of the defect recorded at
#     tidelink/cdc/axi_chiplet_controller.sgdc:60-81.
#
# DOMAIN VERIFIED apb_clk (= app_clk = hclk), not guessed:
#   axi_chiplet_controller.sv:3378  i2c_master_axil u_i2c_master ( .clk(apb_clk)
#   axi_chiplet_controller.sv:3443  i2c_slave_axil_master u_i2c_slave ( .clk(apb_clk)
#   axi_chiplet_controller.sv:3542,3549,3550,3551
#         assign i2c_scl_o = role_is_master ? mst_scl_o : slv_scl_o;   (and _t, sda_*)
# Both cores that drive the mux inputs run on apb_clk, and the mux select
# role_is_master is itself apb_clk-domain (:676-679, from role_locked /
# role_cfg_reg). apb_clk is tied to hclk at the instance
# (tidelink_top.sv:2815), so app_clk models it — the same single-object
# modelling the clock block at the top of §7a already relies on.
# The pins then pass STRAIGHT THROUGH tidelink_top to the chiplet ports with no
# intervening logic (tidelink_top.sv:3028-3032), so this declaration agrees with
# §5 rather than contradicting it.
abstract_port -ports "i2c_scl_o"             -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "i2c_scl_t"             -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "i2c_sda_o"             -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "i2c_sda_t"             -clock app_clk
# [+] nego_error_irq / train_fail_irq_o — auto-negotiation status interrupts,
#     both routed into the d2d_irq vector (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:1033-1034).
abstract_port -ports "nego_error_irq"        -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "train_fail_irq_o"      -clock app_clk
# [+] interrupt — the Wlink interrupt, tidelink_top.sv:3043 -> wlink_irq -> d2d_irq[8].
abstract_port -ports "interrupt"             -clock app_clk

# I2C sideband AXI slave.
abstract_port -ports "s_i2c_axi_*"           -clock app_clk

# [+] data_mode_o — the FCSM>=4 strobe that gates TideChart's root election
#     (src/rtl/nanosoc_eth_chiplet.sv:897,985). Domain VERIFIED apb_clk, not
#     link: it is driven from sync_obs_fcsm_state_1
#     (axi_chiplet_controller.sv:6404), the far side of an apb_clk 2-FF sync
#     (:1985), and the port comment states the domain outright (:504). This one
#     mattered: if it were link-domain, the path into u_tidechart would be an
#     unsynchronised crossing and declaring it app_clk would have hidden it.
abstract_port -ports "data_mode_o"           -clock app_clk

# [+] The four Bug-A FCSM observation taps. All four are the far side of the
#     same apb_clk 2-FF sync chain (axi_chiplet_controller.sv:6394-6398,
#     sync_obs_* registers). They are dangling in this build — tidelink_top.sv:
#     805-809 declares the receiving wires and nothing reads them — so the
#     domain cannot mask a crossing either way; declared for completeness.
abstract_port -ports "obs_a2l_replay_link_valid_o" -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "obs_fe_rx_credit_max_o[*]"   -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "obs_fe_rx_is_full_o"         -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "obs_a2l_replay_app_valid_o"  -clock app_clk

# IDELAY control and the v2 eye-visibility surface — all driven from / returned
# to hclk-domain register blocks at tidelink_top.
abstract_port -ports "idelay_rst"                -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "swi_eye_lane_sel_i[*]"     -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "swi_eye_dwell_us_i[*]"     -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "swi_eye_ctrl_i[*]"         -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_score_idx_i[*]"        -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_status_o[*]"           -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_score_data_o[*]"       -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_score_lane_passed_o"   -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_score_best_o[*]"       -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_score_best_slip_o[*]"  -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_score_best_phase_o[*]" -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_last_slip_o[*]"        -clock app_clk
abstract_port -ports "eye_last_lane_fault_o[*]"  -clock app_clk

# --- link_rx_clk_o (recovered RX word) domain --------------------------------
# The lane checker lives entirely in the recovered-RX-clock domain. The two
# control inputs are CONSUMED there — they are the outputs of the 2-FF/3-FF
# synchroniser chains inside u_gpio_phy_apb_regs that already run on
# link_rx_clk — so declaring them app_clk would invent a crossing at this
# boundary that the synchronisers have already closed.
abstract_port -ports "lane_lock_thresh_i[*]"    -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_clear_noise_i"       -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_mismatch_pulse_o[*]" -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_wire_status_o[*]"    -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_dist_raw_o[*]"       -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_dist_voted_o[*]"     -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_dwell_min_dist_o[*]" -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_noise_min_o[*]"      -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_noise_max_o[*]"      -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_noise_mean_o[*]"     -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_noise_current_o[*]"  -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_canary_pass_o[*]"    -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "lane_canary_valid_o[*]"   -clock link_rx_clk_o

# --- BUILD-CONDITIONAL PORTS — READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING FLISTS --------------
# The three ports below exist ONLY when TIDELINK_PHY_V2 is defined
# (axi_chiplet_controller.sv:522-534, an `ifdef arm inside the module header).
# The ASIC filelist this setup defaults to defines it:
#   build/chip/flist/tidelink_asic.flist:3   +define+TIDELINK_PHY_V2
# Point this .prj at a V1 filelist and these three become unmatched names, which
# is SGDC_abstract_port02 FATAL -> exit code 7 -> a run that reports "0 clocks /
# 0 crossings" and looks like a tool problem. DELETE THIS BLOCK if you do that.
#
# obs_epoch_anchored_o / obs_epoch_span_o are link_rx-domain pass-throughs from
# Wlink's deskew; the receiving APB slave 2-FF-syncs them into apb_clk itself
# (axi_chiplet_controller.sv:4692-4702 and the port comment at :526-528), so the
# link_rx_clk_o domain is the correct declaration and the sync is the thing the
# report should recognise.
abstract_port -ports "obs_epoch_anchored_o" -clock link_rx_clk_o
abstract_port -ports "obs_epoch_span_o[*]"  -clock link_rx_clk_o
# xhb_sub_obs_word_i is driven from tidelink_top's hclk-domain XHB500 ahb_sub
# witness word (tidelink_top.sv:1749,3112).
abstract_port -ports "xhb_sub_obs_word_i[*]" -clock app_clk

# --- B7-ROUND2 ADDITION: scan_out --------------------------------------------
# [+] scan_out was in the "deliberately not declared" list below, on the grounds
#     that DFT is case-analysed at the top level. That is true of the scan
#     INPUTS (§3 pins scan_mode / scan_shift / scan_clk / scan_in to 0). It does
#     NOT reach a black-box OUTPUT: SpyGlass has no model inside the stopped
#     module through which to propagate the constant, so it defaulted the pin to
#     user_ref_clk and reported a user_ref_clk -> sys_hclk crossing against the
#     §5 declaration of the chiplet scan_out port. B7 baseline §6 group A,
#     finding 1.
#
# WHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED HERE. This does NOT claim to know which internal
# flop terminates the controller's scan chain — the module is stopped, so that
# cannot be established from this run, and the chain's last stage could sit in
# any of the controller's internal domains. What it does claim is narrower and
# checkable:
#   (a) The pin is a PURE FEEDTHROUGH from here to a chiplet primary output —
#       axi_chiplet_controller.scan_out -> tidelink_top.sv:3040 -> the chiplet
#       scan_out port. No flop, no gate, nothing to be metastable.
#   (b) With scan_shift / scan_mode / scan_clk / scan_in all case-analysed to 0
#       (§3), no functional path exists on it in mission mode.
# So the two ends of one logic-free wire had contradictory domains, and the
# report was measuring the contradiction, not the design. Making them agree
# retires the artefact without hiding anything, because there is nothing on the
# path to hide.
#
# REVISIT IF the DFT case analysis in §3 is ever removed, or if the controller
# is un-black-boxed (B8) — at that point the chain's real terminal domain
# becomes visible and this line should be replaced by it, not kept.
abstract_port -ports "scan_out"              -clock app_clk

# --- Deliberately NOT declared on this black box -----------------------------
# Listed so the omission is a decision, not an oversight:
#   apb_clk, app_clk, user_hsclk   clocks (app_clk declared above; the other two
#                                  alias to it / to user_ref_clk at the instance)
#   scan_mode, scan_asyncrst_ctrl, scan_clk, scan_shift, scan_in
#                                  DFT, case-analysed at the top level
#
#   poresetn, hresetn              resets, declared at the top level.
#       READ THIS BEFORE "FIXING" IT. These two pins are ALSO silently defaulted
#       to user_ref_clk, by exactly the mechanism group A above describes — and
#       they are the source of TWO of the five findings the B7 baseline classed
#       REAL (findings 32/33, §5 rows 32-33):
#         u_soc.u_chip_core.u_cpu_0.u_core_prmu.u_rstctrl.u_sys_hresetn_sync.rst_sync2_n
#           [sys_fclk] -> u_tidelink.u_chiplet_controller(.../hresetn) [user_ref_clk]
#         ...u_sys_poresetn_sync.rst_sync2_n
#           [sys_fclk] -> u_tidelink.u_chiplet_controller(.../poresetn) [user_ref_clk]
#       It is tempting to add `abstract_port -ports "hresetn" -clock app_clk`
#       here and watch two more findings vanish. DO NOT. Unlike the four I2C
#       pins, these are INPUTS that fan out to MORE THAN ONE internal domain
#       inside the stopped module: §2 of this file records that hresetn feeds
#       app_clk_reset (axi_chiplet_controller.sv:2686) while poresetn feeds
#       wlink_por_reset (:2681), and the role_locked argument in §2 shows a
#       third, recovered-RX regime. Declaring either one app_clk would collapse
#       a real multi-domain reset-distribution question into "same domain" and
#       delete it from the report.
#       The user_ref_clk default is arbitrary — but it errs toward
#       OVER-reporting, which is the safe direction, and it lands these two in
#       the same group H reset-distribution bucket as finding 30 where they
#       belong. Resolving them properly needs the controller's internal reset
#       domains, i.e. B8. Left defaulted ON PURPOSE.
#   pad_clk_tx, pad_tx, pad_clk_rx, pad_rx, idelay_ref_clk
#                                  chiplet pads, declared at the top level and
#                                  propagated straight through tidelink_top
#   role_strap_i, nego_priority_i, puf_seed, puf_ready, mask_hs_bypass_i,
#   apb_debug_unlock_i             straps, quasi_static at the top level (§4)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7b. XHB500 AHB<->AXI bridges
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both variants are single-clock designs: one `clk` input
# (xhb500_ahb_to_axi_bridge_chiplet_slv.sv:26,
#  xhb500_axi_to_ahb_bridge_chiplet_mst.sv:28) and everything else synchronous
# to it. Instantiated at tidelink_top.sv:2477 (u_xhb_sub) and :2564 (u_xhb_mng),
# both on hclk. Wildcarding all ports onto that one clock is safe precisely
# because there is only one clock — it is NOT safe for a multi-domain block, and
# that is why the DAP is not treated this way (see cdc/README.md §5).
current_design xhb500_ahb_to_axi_bridge_chiplet_slv
clock -name clk -domain sys_fclk_domain -period 10.0
abstract_port -ports "*" -clock clk

current_design xhb500_axi_to_ahb_bridge_chiplet_mst
clock -name clk -domain sys_fclk_domain -period 10.0
abstract_port -ports "*" -clock clk

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7c. Compiled memory macros
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# rf_01k / rf_08k / rf_16k / rf_32k are the TSMC65 compiled single-port register
# files (sl_sram.v:57,77,97,117,154; ethmac_sram.v:51). Their Verilog models are
# NOT in any filelist this build reads — Genus takes them from .lib — so
# SpyGlass already sees them as undefined modules, i.e. implicit black boxes.
# The stop_module entry in the .prj makes that explicit and keeps the behaviour
# stable if someone ever adds the models.
#
# Port set verified against /research/precompiled_mems/TSMC65/rf_01k/rf_01k.v:83
#   rf_01k (VDD, VSS, Q, CLK, CEN, WEN, A, D, EMA, EMAW, GWEN, RET1N)
# Single CLK, everything else synchronous to it.
#
# NOT CONSTRAINED HERE, deliberately. `current_design` on a module SpyGlass has
# never read is not something to assert without testing, and the memories are
# single-clock so an unbound black box cannot create a cross-domain path through
# one. If the first run reports crossings AT a memory boundary, the fix is four
# blocks of the form:
#     current_design rf_01k
#     clock -name CLK -domain sys_fclk_domain -period 10.0
#     abstract_port -ports "*" -clock CLK
# with the domain set per instance-site rather than globally — the ethmac RX FIFO
# memories sit in the MII domains, not the fabric.
# TODO(cdc): confirm on the first run; see cdc/README.md §7.
